A||N||25||rigdonhome||¿¿¿ The Fiero's Snow-Going Capabilities ???||||5||
Z||000000||rigdonhome||11-14-2001||01:08 PM||||OK, I know there are a lot of you Fiero fanatics out there that drive your cars year-round in all sorts of climates...  I may give up my 4x4 as a cost-saving measure and would then have to drive my '88 GT year-round in the Indianapolis area.  Keep in mind that I'm a very talented driver (a little arrogant too...) and used to drive a 300hp Chevelle in Wisconsin winters.<P>I would guess that the Fiero would get decent traction with the engine in the back, but might want to push the front end in slick situations.<P>Any advice on whether or not I should expect to be able to drive the Fiero year-round would be greatly appreciated!  I doubt the snow gets deeper than 2-3inches at any given time, but it DOES get slick here with freezing rain and sleet...<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ferrari_cdn||11-14-2001||01:25 PM||||I have never driven a Fiero in the snow, (I just got mine a few months ago).  But by word of mouth, Fieros are easy cars to drive in snow, but, it's not too hard to go over its limit and get yourself in trouble.  Just take it easy on the throttle on the corners, stop early, etc etc.  Unless you want to have some fun, then find a large empty parking lot and do donuts there.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Oh also, be sure you have good tires.  A friend of mine has a bit worn threads and he said his car is like a tobaggan (sp?) on road.  (It snowed here a few weeks ago but all disappeared).  When it DID snow, it wasn't hard for me to drive it.  But, when I did give it a bit too much gas in a corner, the back end gave out too easily.  (But I caught it just in time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) Just be careful there.  Especially if you're driving a V6.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">   </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero<p>[This message has been edited by Ferrari_cdn (edited 11-14-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000002||camon||11-14-2001||01:39 PM||||I "use to" have an 86 Fiero SE 2m6 and it had no problem getting moving in the snow but it had a big problem steering in the snow!<P>I was going like 20mph with about an inch or so of snow on the ground with the stock GoodYear Eagle GT tires. Well I went to make a turn and the car continued to go straight and tapped a guard rail. Luckily I didn't hit hard and the only damage that resulted was a pushed in nose (no other damage).<P>So as far as there ability in the snow....I question it. Luckily I have a 4 wheel drive sidekick that I will drive when weather calls for it! <P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||1
Z||000003||SKIDMARK||11-14-2001||01:41 PM||||Well, I drove my 4 cyl daily driver all winter last year. We get some pretty deep snow in the eastern burbs of Cleveland but I didn't have any trouble once I got good rubber on the car and all 4 wheels pointed in the same direction (alignment). I also pushed it past it's limits in the empty parking lot at work just so I knew what to expect. Like you said, the front end washes out and you can't steer.  Just don't expect it to go well in deep snow. <P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||14
Z||000004||stsmithgt||11-14-2001||01:52 PM||||Don't expect to be in a hurry either.<BR>Too much gas = sit and spin.<BR>Too much brake=sliiiiiddddeeee....<BR>I took mine up to Lake Tahoe last year to go skiing.  Interesting slip, I'm mean trip. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>My buddies new Subaru kicked my but when it came to getting around up there.  We took his car to the ski lodge and "stuck" mine in the garage at the cabin once I was able to get up the driveway.  I had brand new tires too.<P>------------------<BR>My work of art 4..s..money. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4money.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||frontal lobe||11-14-2001||01:53 PM||||I live in Wisconsin.  Snow isn't TOO bad to drive in, but you MUST drive slowly and anticipate ahead of time.  Not a fun driving experience, but do-able.<P>WATCH IT! on the ice, or even real wet.  Especially with the v6 if you take a turn and get on the gas too soon too hard, look out!  Expect the back end to rotate VERY easily.||||reg||1
Z||000006||GSXPete||11-14-2001||02:20 PM||||i was wondering the same thing. i'm trying to decide if i should wait untill the spring to get the car, or buy it now and try to make it through the winter. i have that option becuase the fiero i'm gonna get isnt really trying to be sold...he said if i want it, i can buy it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> does having 4 snow tires help alot? i drove an AWD eclipse last winter, so i really know nothing about driving a fiero, or any RWD in the snow for that matter. i'm gonna miss going for a drive in 16 inches of snow and not getting stuck anywhere. i'd climb the steepest hills with ease, passing everyone that was all over the road trying to get up...hehe. <P>o yea..ya cant beat AWD donuts either  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> hehe||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||11-14-2001||02:34 PM||||My first 86 SE Was driven in many winters. (the second one only saw 2 winters) Even from New York to Chicago. First make sure you have decent all-weather tires. I was using BFGoodrich T/A's Never had a problem with snow less than 3" deep. Yes you do need to turn corners carefully, Adding 50lbs of sand in the front helps. In greater than 3" of snow, like any car you need to use caution. The front likes to float a little. I've driven mine to snow as deep as 8". The snow was coming up the hood and I basically plowed the road to a 6" height (Stock height of a Fiero) Fiero's will go straight thru most any snow. While most RWD vehicles would be spinning tires at traffic lights, I would drive past them with ease.<BR>I think the Fiero is better in snow than lots of other cars I've had. The only thing better is 4-wheel drive/AWD vehicles.<BR>As for Ice, I don't know any vehicle that does well on it. Even a tank would slide on ice.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||onfire||11-14-2001||02:38 PM||||Fiero's are great in the snow. This is the first Fiero I ever owned, it was an 85 GT. It was my daily driver for 2 and a half years. I never had any problems in the snow or the rain, and I live where it rains 9 months out of the year. This picture was taken in North Bend, WA going up some logging roads. It got really deep after about 2 miles. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/snow1.jpg"> <P>You just have to watch out for other people. And leave about twice the distance between you and other drivers. Then you normally would. Especially braking distance. <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/onfiresigtoo.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000009||rogergarrison||11-14-2001||02:52 PM||||Ive got caught out in sudden snow & ice storms in my kit. It actually goes pretty well and long as its not deep enough to drag on the bottom (4 or 5 inches)It does need a sandbag or something in the front. Its so lite, it tends to skate around a little. I dont have one, but id think an auto car is much better at getting going in snow than a standard too. Most Fieros ive driven seem to have a pretty low ratio 1st gear.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||onfire||11-14-2001||03:08 PM||||I always started out in second on the 4speed.||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfiero||11-14-2001||03:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rigdonhome:<BR><B><BR>Any advice on whether or not I should expect to be able to drive the Fiero year-round would be greatly appreciated!  I doubt the snow gets deeper than 2-3inches at any given time, but it DOES get slick here with freezing rain and sleet...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Fieros do pretty well in the snow, but suck on ice. I would strongly suggest driving something else when it is nasty outside......especially since your GT only has 38K miles!  Why risk messing it up?  Keep the 4x4 for the ice and snow. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fformula88||11-14-2001||03:23 PM||||My father has driven a Fiero year round since 1990 here in Buffalo, where we wish there was only 2-3 inches on the ground as the max.<P>A Fiero can handle snow and slick roads.  You just have to take your time (like in anything else).  Since Indy does not get deep snow hardly ever I'd say you would be fine.  With the weight over the drive wheels, and going easy on the throttle, your not going to get stuck.  You might also want to get some snow tires if it really bugs you but my father always had all-season radials.<P>I haven't really driven my Formula in snow except when I needed the garage to repair other vehicles so I parked it out on the street.  The V6 was a lot more prone to spinning its wheels than dads old 4-banger.  So you'd have to take it easy.  Also I have performance rubber on the car too, which won't bite into the snow.<P>Biggest key, take you time and go slow when its slick.  This goes for driving 4X4's too, as I always see more of them than anything off the road in ditches after the first snowfall!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000013||SwissFormula||11-14-2001||05:43 PM||||Fiero & 8 year old Goodrich Comp T/A's & snow = funny handlig<P>I tried this two years ago, get moving was not the problem, and when driving slow enough it will get you where you want. But, little to fast in that corner, and youf Fiero is gonna show you some combinations of understeer & oversteer you've never seen before  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>First time it happend to me was in a right turn. I thought i will hit that house on the LEFT side, the car going almost sraight, half a second later i was back on MY side of the road, turned 45 degrees more to the right than needed. I was driving slow, now stopped, lucky there was no traffic so went home. Slowly...<BR>However, i believe that its save & fun to drive in snow with a REAL GOOD set of tires. I never tried because i got me a cheap car for "winter & bad weather driving" after my fiero almost scared me to death... That also keeps my fiero away from that car eating salt.<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula, T-Top<BR>1 of 20 Fieros in Switzerland||||reg||1
Z||000014||crgkstnr||11-14-2001||06:35 PM||||Rig,<BR>tires, tires, tires.  I drive my 87gt through the Michigan winter.  I've got another set of wheels and tires for winter.  That way there isn't compromise performance from one set of tires.  Get a winter set of wheels and some skinny tires with appropriate tread which sink into the snow.<BR>With appropriate tires there's no reason a<BR>Fiero can't handle winter as well as any car.<P>Well ok, there is the clearance issue.  It might be a good idea to protect the lower part of the radiator.  It's not a good way to plow snow.<P>Craig<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||rigdonhome||11-14-2001||07:38 PM||||Lots of great advice!!!<P>I AM worried about the salt eating what little metal my Fiero has, so I will probably buy a $200 beater 4x4 a buddy has that needs a little work...  That way I can part out my TPI V8 4x4 S10 Blazer that is now used mosly for trails!<P>Keep the good advice and stories coming!!!<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000016||mrfiero||11-14-2001||09:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rigdonhome:<BR><B>Lots of great advice!!!<P>I AM worried about the salt eating what little metal my Fiero has, so I will probably buy a $200 beater 4x4 a buddy has that needs a little work...  That way I can part out my TPI V8 4x4 S10 Blazer that is now used mosly for trails!<P>Keep the good advice and stories coming!!!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Very good idea.  I drive a Fiero everyday (it's my only car) and have had no real problems driving in the snow, but like I said above.....it isn't worth the risk for you considering the low miles your GT has on it.  Not only the salt/rust aspect, but also bumps and bruises from other people sliding into you!<P>I have a 16K mile black '88 GT and would not think about driving it in the snow.....heck, I don't drive it when it rains!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000017||sqoach||11-14-2001||10:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B> <BR>Not only the salt/rust aspect, but also bumps and bruises from other people sliding into you!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There's the key. <I>Other</I> people not knowing what they're doing. Heck, a lot of people around here don't even know how to drive when the weather is nice!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>That's my main concern, anyway. Gotta watch out for them youngun's and the elderly around my town  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><BR>(No offense to anyone here)<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigsqoach2.gif">              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">               <BR>^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 SC 2.5L 5spd</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Neal||11-14-2001||11:04 PM||||I heard an interesting stat for my city a while ago, that there are actually MORE accidents in Calgarys summer months than winter months.  Go figure, roads are nice so they drive like idiots...<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B> There's the key. <I>Other</I> people not knowing what they're doing. Heck, a lot of people around here don't even know how to drive when the weather is nice!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>That's my main concern, anyway. Gotta watch out for them youngun's and the elderly around my town  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Indyrestoguy_JR||11-14-2001||11:29 PM||||Just remember to watch it when it downshifts. I put one in a ditch on an unexpected downshift. Being from the Indy area it gets a little hairy when it ices over bad but does not do bad at all in snow and moderate slush. About the salt, if you wash it and make sure it gets dry (heated garage) it shouldn't rust. ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Fformula88||11-15-2001||12:26 PM||||Salt will eat it no matter how often you clean it underneath.  You might be able to slow its progress, but its not going to stop it.<P>The Fiero's achilles heel is those floor pan caps underneath.  Those things are very suseptable to salt, and once they start rusting it quickly spreads to the rest of the underside of the Fiero.  That Fiero I sad my father drove for 10 winters... it now has huge holes in the floorboards from this.  The rust spread outward from those center caps, and once there is a small hole the moisture gets trapped between the sheetmetal and the underside of the carpeting.  Holes grow quickly!<P>As for garaging... I have heard it is bad to put a car in the garage in the winter if you drive it on salty roads.  The reason was that in the garage, it keeps the car warm which melts the frozen ice/snow throughout the vehicle.  This keeps the salt in a liquid state which makes it more volatile metal.  If snow/ice was frozen, as it is on a car out of doors, the salt won't be able to flow to the car, instead being trapped on/in the ice.<P>From experience, and maybe its just coincidence, but I do believe this.  I have used the garage in the winter in the past and had terrible rust problems develop on the car (a 1991 Probe).  This was back in 95-97.  To this day I still see 91 Probes with little to no rust on them, and my 97 Probe which I have left outside in the winters since I bought it was yet to show any signs of rust. But I will never park a car in the garage in the winter if I am driving it and want it to last a while!  ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Mach10||11-15-2001||12:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ferrari_cdn:<BR><B>I have never driven a Fiero in the snow, (I just got mine a few months ago).  But by word of mouth, Fieros are easy cars to drive in snow, but, it's not too hard to go over its limit and get yourself in trouble.  Just take it easy on the throttle on the corners, stop early, etc etc.  Unless you want to have some fun, then find a large empty parking lot and do donuts there.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Oh also, be sure you have good tires.  A friend of mine has a bit worn threads and he said his car is like a tobaggan (sp?) on road.  (It snowed here a few weeks ago but all disappeared).  When it DID snow, it wasn't hard for me to drive it.  But, when I did give it a bit too much gas in a corner, the back end gave out too easily.  (But I caught it just in time   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) Just be careful there.  Especially if you're driving a V6.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>That would be me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>My tires aren't worn, they are simply GARBAGE. They aren't bad on dry pavement, but on wet they are SCARY, and on Ice... Yeah... It's a toboggan. <P>However, I've driven OTHER v6 fieros on snow/ice, and they aren't much different than any other car. Just don't hammer the gas through icy corners, and it's fairly predictable. In my case, I have 4 sets of balljoints going, and crappy tires. Due to go in for BJs next week, new tires the following, and a couple of 50lb bags of "Traction Control" (aka sandbags  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )up front to keep the spare company  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>As with any car, if you **** around, it's going to bite you. <P>||||reg||10
Z||000022||William Federle||11-15-2001||01:14 PM||||My 1984 SE has about a gozillian miles on it and I drive it year round. From what I've seen on the forum, the best tires in the snow are 13" tires. That's what I have on mine. I drive to Fond du Lac from Milwaukee every day and I usually try to go as fast as conditions and the law permits.<P> In the winter, if I feel the rear end begin to have a little sideways motion, I back off on the gas. For any conditions, there's a safe speed. Just don't push it any faster when the car starts to warn you. Also, be careful on bridges and when going through underpasses.||||reg||1
Z||000023||MrPAW||11-15-2001||01:48 PM||||I have had two Fieros that I drove in the winter - one had run of the mill all season tires and the other had eagle gt somethings(the cheaper ones).<P>On a light coating of snow (&lt;1") they were both fine.<P>On heavier snow, ice and slush the performance was much as the others have reported above:<P>Starting - no problem with traction.<P>Braking - allow a little extra space and you're fine.<P>Steering - Slim to NONE! at any speed above 10mph the car definately preffered going in a straight line to making a corner.<P>I live in Fort Wayne so our driving conditions should be similar.<P><BR>On the other hand I worked for a Pontiac dealer in 85 and we got in an SE for service. It had snow treads on all four corners (I don't remember what brand).  This car had absolutely no problem in the 9" of snow we had that day.<P>With all seasons so popular I'm not even sure you can buy snow tires anymore?<P>Paul<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Fformula88||11-15-2001||05:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MrPAW:<BR><B><BR>With all seasons so popular I'm not even sure you can buy snow tires anymore?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Plenty of snow tires to be had out there with some large strides in technology!  Some of the studless tires perform very well on ice.  Not as good as studs but WAY better than all seasons.<P>In fact, a large percentage of "all season" tires really aren't good snow tires at all!  It is how they are tested.<P>The new studless snows are tires like Bridgestone Blizzaks, Michelin Actic Alpins and Pilot Alpins, and some others.  The Miata owners swear by Nokkian winter tires!  ||||reg||1
Z||000025||Mach10||11-15-2001||06:14 PM||||I'm goin' for Studdlies  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Not gonna **** around with my Prized Material Possession.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||10
